  <p class="page-intro" id="privacy-summary"><strong>The short version.</strong> This site does not
  track you. It sets no cookies, keeps no account, and does not sell or share
  anything about you. It counts how many people visit each page, without
  knowing who they are. If you email us, we keep the email for as long as it
  takes to deal with. That is very nearly the whole of it; the rest of this
  page says the same thing in more detail.</p>

  <p class="mono">Last updated 29 August 2026.</p>

  <h2>Who runs this site</h2>
  <p>largs.scot is run by Ian McColm, a Largs resident, as a volunteer. Under
  data protection law that makes him the “data controller” — the person
  responsible for how this site handles information. You can reach him at
  <a href="mailto:hello@largs.scot">hello@largs.scot</a>.</p>

  <h2>What happens when you visit a page</h2>
  <p><strong>Your connection is handled by Cloudflare.</strong> Every website
  is served from somewhere; this one is served by Cloudflare. When your device
  asks for a page, Cloudflare sees your IP address and which page you asked
  for, as any website's host does. Cloudflare keeps connection logs for
  security under its own policy; we do not see them.</p>

  <p><strong>We count visits without identifying you.</strong> We use
  Cloudflare Web Analytics to see how many people look at each page. It uses
  no cookies, builds no profile of you, and does not follow you to other
  websites. We see totals — “the roadworks page was viewed 140 times this
  week” — and nothing about any individual.</p>

  <p><strong>Nothing else is loaded from anywhere else.</strong> The
  typefaces, images and stylesheets all come from this site, so no request
  leaves your browser for another company while you read. The only exception
  is the maps.</p>

  <p><strong>Maps.</strong> The roadworks and aircraft pages show maps. The
  map images come from <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org">OpenStreetMap</a>,
  a non-profit mapping project, so your device requests them from
  OpenStreetMap's servers, which see your IP address. No other page loads
  anything from a third party.</p>

  <h2>Cookies</h2>
  <p>None. This site sets no cookies and stores nothing on your device. That
  is why there is no cookie banner.</p>

  <h2>When you email us</h2>
  <p>If you write to hello@, events@, corrections@ or letter@largs.scot, we
  receive your email address, your name if you give it, and whatever you
  write. We use it to reply and to act on what you have said — adding an
  event, checking a correction. Email is handled by Fastmail, on servers in
  the European Union.</p>

  <p>We keep correspondence for as long as it takes to deal with, and a
  little beyond in case there is a follow-up. Corrections are different: we
  keep a record of what was reported, what we checked and what changed,
  because that record is part of being accountable for what the site says. It
  does not name you unless you ask it to.</p>

  <p>Whatever you send is used only for the reason you sent it. Your address
  will not be added to any list.</p>

  <h2>The Largs Letter</h2>
  <p>When the monthly email starts, you will be able to subscribe by giving
  your email address. We will use it for the Letter and nothing else. Every
  edition will carry an unsubscribe link, and unsubscribing removes your
  address entirely. The list will not be shared with anyone. Until the Letter
  starts, there is no list.</p>

  <h2>Names in the council record</h2>
  <p>Council corner records what Largs Community Council has raised and what
  became of it, built from the approved minutes published by North Ayrshire
  Council. Those minutes are a public record and they name community
  councillors — people acting in a public role, in a public meeting, whose
  decisions are the point of the record. Members of the public who raise
  matters are not named. How the register is compiled, and how to report
  something wrong in it, is set out on the
  <a href="/council/how-this-works/">How this record works</a> page.</p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>Data protection law gives you rights over information about you: to see
  what is held, to have it corrected or deleted, and to object to how it is
  used. On this site the honest answer to “what do you hold about me” is
  usually “nothing” — but if you have emailed us, or subscribed to the
  Letter, write to <a href="mailto:hello@largs.scot">hello@largs.scot</a> and
  we will tell you what we have and delete it if you wish.</p>

  <p>If you are not satisfied with how we have handled your information, you
  can complain to the <a href="https://ico.org.uk">Information Commissioner's
  Office</a>, or phone them on 0303 123 1113.</p>

  <h2 id="copyright">Copyright and reuse</h2>
  <p>© largs.scot — Ian McColm, 2026. Photographs © Alison Swinburne.</p>

  <p><strong>The words and data are yours to reuse.</strong> Anything written
  or compiled here — the council register, the plain-English summaries, the
  bin rotation, these pages — may be copied, quoted and republished, including
  by community groups, the Community Council and the press, provided largs.scot
  is credited. No permission is needed and none has to be asked for. Much of it
  summarises public business from published records, and a record of what a
  public body decided is not something to keep to ourselves.</p>

  <p><strong>The photographs and artwork are not.</strong> Alison Swinburne's
  photographs, and the illustrations drawn for this site, are separate work
  and remain reserved. If you would like to use one, write to
  <a href="mailto:hello@largs.scot">hello@largs.scot</a> and ask — the answer
  is often yes.</p>

  <p><strong>Material from other sources keeps its own terms.</strong> Weather
  and tide figures come from Open-Meteo under CC BY 4.0; roadworks come from
  the Scottish Road Works Register; council papers and minutes are North
  Ayrshire Council's, and the typefaces are under the SIL Open Font Licence.
  Each is credited where it appears, and reusing it means following that
  licence rather than this one.</p>

  <h2>Changes to this page</h2>
  <p>If the site starts doing something new with information, this page will
  be updated before it does, and the date at the top will change. Nothing
  will be added quietly.</p>
